{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqbc3zun3l4","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"Bench runs against isolated daemon with a frozen graph snapshot for reproducibility","summary":"Concrete fix (2026-06-12, user-proposed) for the KB-churn bench-reproducibility problem [[note-mqbbndrgd1k]]. Today the bench gated arm RAGs against the LIVE churning KB: bench-heldout.js spawns claude with bench/mcp-on.json which runs mcp-graph.js, and mcp-graph.js connects to a daemon at ORCH_PORT (default live :8787) — so live KB churn moves the bench target run-to-run. FIX: boot an ISOLATED daemon on a PRIVATE port with isolated CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA and a workspace pointed at a FROZEN .graph snapshot dir, then run bench-heldout.js with ORCH_PORT=<private> in the spawned claude env so its mcp-graph.js hits the isolated daemon, not live. Gives: reproducible (same KB every run), isolated (no live-KB journal pollution, immune to live churn), craftable (seed the exact targeting note per candidate into the snapshot). Infra already exists — wiring not new substrate: test/smoke.sh already boots an isolated daemon on a private port + sets CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA + POSTs /workspace; the daemon loads overlay/.graph from ORCH_WORKSPACE; mcp-graph.js honors ORCH_PORT (line 10). KEEP RAG (not a DAG edge) on the gated arm — the bench tests the GATE; a DAG context edge to the targeting note would force-inject and bypass the gate, defeating the test. Make it opt-in (flag/env) so existing live-bench behavior is preserved.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-12T19:43:40.607Z","vec":[-0.12739534676074982,0.006651957985013723,-0.036431338638067245,0.013870366849005222,0.03640134632587433,-0.08786692470312119,-0.11395230889320374,0.0022235014475882053,-0.037594906985759735,0.053372349590063095,0.0023772651329636574,-0.0069372523576021194,0.02974857948720455,-0.09597010165452957,-0.007604850921779871,-0.054624494165182114,0.07284276187419891,-0.04482128843665123,0.06604908406734467,-0.04340352118